const s=(id,label,title,description,refs,extra={})=>({id,label,title,description,reference:'d03-interconnection-queues',sources:refs,...extra});
export const scenes = [
  s('queue-purpose','The race to connect','What does a place in the queue actually buy?', 'Title slide for the unnumbered case study between Chapter 4 and Chapter 5. The two logos identify the grid operators compared: ERCOT, which serves most of Texas, and PJM, which coordinates a multi-state region in the eastern United States.',[sources.ercot,sources.dominion]),
  s('chips-need-power','Chips are not power','Buying the GPUs does not get them powered.', 'Delivered hardware does not run until a building with a power connection exists. In October 2025 Microsoft’s chief executive said his constraint was power and powered buildings, not chip supply, and described chips sitting in inventory that he could not plug in. This is one company’s dated statement, not a measurement of idle GPUs across the industry, and his constraint includes data-center construction as well as the utility connection. A grid connection is what ERCOT’s large-load process and, in PJM, the utility’s process decide.',[sources.nadella,sources.microsoftCall]),
  s('ercot-pipeline','ERCOT: read the stage','Most of ERCOT’s 474.7 GW has no approved study.', 'ERCOT Senate presentation, July 29, 2026, data as of June 2026: every large-load request ERCOT has tracked since 2022, by current status, as requested load by 2033. It is not this year’s applications and not ERCOT’s load forecast. One bar, three groups: 284.3 GW “no studies submitted” (ERCOT has not received the utility’s study, or reviewed megawatts and did not approve them); 135.5 GW with a study under ERCOT review; 55.0 GW with studies approved or further (9.9 + 36.0 + 3.2 + 5.9). Of the last group, 5.9 GW is observed energized: all-time non-simultaneous peak consumption, each site’s peak added together rather than load at one moment. ERCOT says roughly 90% of the requested load is data centers. ERCOT’s monthly overviews for the same summer give slightly different totals, so the numbers stay pinned to this dated deck. None of the gaps is a cancellation rate.',[sources.ercot,sources.ercotDefinition]),
  s('site-options','One project, several requests','One campus can keep several sites in play.', 'Original teaching example: one 1 GW business deployment considers three mutually exclusive sites. Three 1 GW requests add to 3 GW, but the specified plan builds at most one campus; choosing one site later withdraws the other two requests without cancelling the planned deployment. This is an incentive mechanism, not an estimate of duplicated ERCOT capacity. Relatively low early commitment can encourage options; SB6 requires disclosure of substantially similar requests.',[sources.sb6]),
  s('ercot-analyst','SemiAnalysis: screen the requests','A queue total is not a buildout forecast.', 'Unmodified public SemiAnalysis figure from June 18, 2026. It compares April ERCOT requests (410 GW total, around 357 GW data centers) with its own 45.4 GW tracked data-center buildout through 2030 Q4. The phantom/duplicative classification belongs to the publisher’s model, not an ERCOT audit. Different horizons and tracking coverage mean the gap does not prove cancellation or fraud.',[sources.saRequests]),
  s('commitment-costs','Study fees and security','New in 2026: a large-load request in ERCOT posts security for every megawatt.', 'This is a change, not how Texas always worked. Before SB 6 (signed June 20, 2025) no ERCOT-wide study fee or per-MW security was in force; each transmission utility ran its own process and set its own study charges and security, so earlier requests were not free. Texas rule 25.194, adopted September 18 and effective October 8, 2026, requires a new request of 75 MW or more to fund an intermediate agreement before ERCOT includes it in a study: a flat $100,000 study fee (unused amount returned after the study) and security of $50,000/MW. A 1,000 MW request means a $50 million face amount, which a qualifying guarantee or letter of credit can satisfy; a customer that withdraws after being allocated capacity can forfeit part of it. At the later standard interconnection agreement, security is the greater of $50,000/MW or allocated system-upgrade costs. Interim steps came first: a March 1, 2026 forecasting rule offered $100,000/MW as one way to show commitment, and ERCOT’s Batch Zero rules (PGRR145, effective July 11, 2026) already required $50,000/MW from projects entering that study on the studied-and-allocated path.',[sources.sb6,sources.security]),
  s('dominion-in-pjm','PJM: who is Dominion?','Dominion is a utility. Its zone is one of 21 inside PJM.', 'PJM’s own transmission-zones map, cropped to the map. PJM Interconnection operates the grid and wholesale market across all or parts of 13 states and Washington, D.C.; it owns no power lines or generators. Dominion Energy Virginia is the main utility in the Dominion zone, which covers most of Virginia and contains Northern Virginia’s data-center cluster; a data center there asks its utility, not PJM, for the connection. The relationship parallels ERCOT and wires utilities such as Oncor, with two differences: Dominion also owns generation and sells retail power, and as of mid-2026 PJM has no central ERCOT-style large-load process. Cooperatives in the same zone, such as NOVEC, report their own data-center load to PJM.',[sources.pjmZones,sources.dominion]),
  s('dominion-contracts','Dominion: contracts by stage','Dominion’s contracts far exceed what its data centers draw.', 'The chart from slide 5 of Dominion Energy’s Q2 2026 earnings presentation, July 31, 2026, cropped to its bars and linked to the full slide: data-center contracted capacity by stage, beside the three stages and the 2025 peak in course typography. July 2026: 32.4 GW substation engineering letters of authorization (SELOA, called ELOA in Dominion’s January 2026 letter to PJM), 9.4 GW construction letters of authorization and 12.0 GW electric service agreements. The engineering stage is a study the customer pays for and is not obliged to follow with construction; construction authorization makes the customer liable for spent costs; the service agreement carries a revenue requirement. The roughly 4 GW coincident data-center peak in 2025 comes from the January 6, 2026 letter. Contract capacity is the most those sites could draw, not what they will draw; the two numbers carry different dates, and the gap is not a measured cancellation rate. These are Dominion Energy’s own contracts; cooperatives in the zone report separately.',[sources.dominionContracts,sources.dominion]),
  s('forecast-filter','From requests to a forecast','Planners do not simply add every requested megawatt.', 'Original ERCOT 2025 forecast figure reproduced in the March 3, 2026 SemiAnalysis article. The 2030 total-system comparison is 208 GW submitted versus 138 GW adjusted. This is a historical forecast vintage, not the current queue or data-center-only demand. A forecast applies assumptions about timing and realization; it does not allocate service rights. Avoid treating the publisher’s broader market-policy argument as established causality.',[sources.saForecast]),
  s('staged-connection','Start within an import limit','A campus can grow while its grid import stays limited.', 'Simplified course redraw of ERCOT’s May 4, 2026 workshop illustration, reproduced by SemiAnalysis June 25: 1,000 MW requested load, 100 MW grid withdrawal and two 500 MW generators. With no local generation the power balance allows only 100 MW; with one generator 600 MW; with both, at most the 1,000 MW load, not 1,100 MW. Network, stability, fuel, permits and operating restrictions can further limit these amounts. This illustrates a staged proposal, not an approved project or automatic right. Loss of a generator cannot be assumed to transfer its entire load to the capped grid connection.',[sources.saStaged]),
  s('btm-gap','SemiAnalysis: the behind-the-meter gap','From 2028, SemiAnalysis expects most new data-center capacity to need its own power.', 'Unmodified public SemiAnalysis figure from June 25, 2026, supplied by the author. Each bar is the publisher’s forecast of US data-center capacity added in that year (annual net additions in GW, not the cumulative fleet): 21 GW in 2026 rising to 84 GW in 2030. Orange is existing grid headroom, exhausted after 2027; green is new grid supply; blue is the residual gap the publisher says must be filled behind the meter: 14.8 GW in 2027, 42.6 GW in 2028. The blue series is a modeled shortfall, not a count of contracted onsite generation. This is an analyst forecast, national rather than specific to ERCOT or PJM.',[sources.saStaged],{pedagogical_role:'synthesis'}),
];
